# Perfect Competition — recommendation

**Recommendation.** Plant 10 tomato, 20 carrot and 30 mesclun beds this season, for a profit of $42,761.66 after fixed costs.

## Why

- **Every bed planted earns more than it costs, and the next one wouldn't.** Tomatoes stop at 10 because the 11th bed costs $9,390.72 to grow and sells for $8,800. Carrots and mesclun would still make money on more beds, but both are already at their bed caps.
- **Labor and land are not what limit us.** The plan uses 60 of 64 beds and 5,277 of 6,480 labor hours. Even with every limit removed, the best plan needs 3.98 of our 4 workers.
- **Growing beyond this pays little.** Removing every limit adds only $2,249.13 (about 5%), because each extra bed earns less than the one before.

## The judgment call

Lift the carrot cap first. The first extra carrot bed is worth $352.49 against $246.47 for mesclun, and carrots stay ahead bed for bed. The four unused beds mean no new land is needed: raising the carrot cap from 20 to 24 adds $1,075.85. Beyond 64 beds, the best extra bed is worth $246.47 a season (the 31st mesclun bed), and each one after that is worth less. I would not buy or rent land that costs more than about $250 a bed per season. The model has no land-cost figure, so that ceiling is the call I'm making without data.

## What would change my answer

The tomato price. The 10th tomato bed clears its cost by only $551.41, so a price drop of more than 6.3% (below about $8,250 a bed) cuts tomatoes to 9 beds. A 20% drop would cut them further.
